On Dec 18, 2007 2:58 PM, Timo Paulssen <timonator@perpetuum-immobile.de>
wrote:

> Mark E. Shoulson wrote:
> > For example, in the sentence "Fred is a doctor,"
> > the subject is "Fred" and the predicate is "is a doctor" ("a doctor"
> > being a predicate nominative, and part of the predicate) whereas the
> > verb is "is."
>
> isn't is only an auxilliary verb anyway? or am i confusing stuff with
> german? ;)


The verb 'to be' *can *be an auxiliary verb (e.g., "Fred *is *becoming a
doctor."), but is the main verb in the sentence above. I'd be dismayed if a
similar distinction weren't made in German ("Fritz ist Arzt" / "Fritz ist
Arzt geworden").
           
